\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Functions of the links within a closed time half}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Use the \vocabk{site reflection}{fdip-007M} on
#{X=Y\times\mathbb Z/(2M)}, with #{M\ge2} and #{Y} a spatial
torus of periods at least three or a point. With time
coordinate #{x_t}, put #{X_+=Y\times\{0,\ldots,M\}},
#{\theta_s(y,x_t)=(y,-x_t)} and #{X_-=\theta_s X_+}.
Let #{E_\pm} consist of both oriented versions of every edge
whose two endpoints lie in #{X_\pm}. Its reverse link is the
inverse of the stored transport on the positive orientation.
Use a measurable class #{\Omega} of represented unitary
connections, with measurable restrictions and link inversion.}

\p{The \newvocab{connection half-algebra} #{\mathcal A_\pm}
consists of bounded complex functions on #{\Omega} measurable
for the sigma-algebra generated by restriction to #{E_\pm}.
For an involutive coordinate reflection #{a}, use the
oriented link pullback #{(aU)(e)=U(ae)}, and on observables
write #{a^*f(U)=f(aU)} whenever the map preserves the class.
Given a probability #{\nu} on #{\Omega} and a measurable
action of #{\theta_s} on it, put
##{(\Theta f)(U)=\overline{f(\theta_s U)},\qquad
 B_\pm(f,g)=\int_\Omega(\Theta f)(U)g(U)\,\extd\nu(U)
 \quad(f,g\in\mathcal A_\pm).}}
